      { role: "doctor", type: "clinical", text: "PROCALCITONIN (PCT) GUIDED ANTIBIOTIC THERAPY is one of the few biomarker-guided strategies with ROBUST EVIDENCE for safely reducing antibiotic exposure: in LOWER RESPIRATORY TRACT INFECTIONS, PCT-guided protocols have been shown in multiple RCTs (ProHOSP, PRORATA trials) to reduce antibiotic use by 30-40% without increasing mortality or complications. The principle: PCT rises rapidly in BACTERIAL infections and falls as the infection is controlled; PCT falling to <0.25 ng/mL (or >80% decline from peak) supports STOPPING antibiotics. Importantly, PCT is LESS reliable in: immunocompromised patients, non-infectious inflammatory states (pancreatitis, major surgery, trauma — all raise PCT without infection). Understanding PCT's STRENGTHS AND LIMITATIONS prevents both over- and under-reliance on this biomarker." },
      { role: "pharmacist", type: "clinical", text: "PHARMACIST-LED ANTIMICROBIAL STEWARDSHIP INTERVENTIONS have the strongest evidence base of any single professional group in stewardship: pharmacist-led IV-to-oral switch programmes, pharmacist-driven de-escalation review, TDM optimisation, and allergy assessment with de-labelling all have robust evidence for reducing antibiotic duration, reducing costs, and reducing resistance selection without compromising outcomes. In settings where dedicated infectious disease physicians are limited (including many African hospitals), THE CLINICAL PHARMACIST IS OFTEN THE PRIMARY STEWARDSHIP PROFESSIONAL — making antimicrobial pharmacist training and recognition a critical healthcare system investment. Building pharmacist confidence in CHALLENGING PRESCRIPTIONS and RECOMMENDING ALTERNATIVES is central to effective stewardship." },
      { role: "nurse", type: "clinical", text: "NURSES AS STEWARDSHIP PARTNERS: key nursing contributions to antimicrobial stewardship include: (1) CULTURE COLLECTION — taking blood cultures BEFORE antibiotic administration, ensuring correct technique (sterile, two sites), and labelling precisely; (2) ANTIBIOTIC TIMING — ensuring antibiotics are given at the prescribed time (particularly critical for time-sensitive infections like neutropenic sepsis, meningitis), and documenting actual administration time (not just prescribed time); (3) REVIEW PROMPTING — flagging when antibiotic courses have continued beyond the review date without documented reassessment; (4) IV-TO-ORAL CRITERIA ASSESSMENT — identifying when patients meet criteria for oral switch and escalating to medical review; (5) PATIENT EDUCATION — counselling patients on why their viral illness does not require antibiotics." },
      { role: "labtech", type: "practical", text: "THE LABORATORY'S CENTRAL STEWARDSHIP ROLE is often underappreciated: (1) ANTIBIOGRAM PRODUCTION — compiling local resistance data and distributing to clinical teams is a fundamental stewardship function; antibiograms should be updated at least annually and made easily accessible; (2) CASCADE REPORTING — reporting only appropriate antibiotics for each organism (e.g. NOT reporting fluoroquinolone susceptibility for E. coli in a patient with no indication for fluoroquinolone use, to prevent reflexive selection of broad-spectrum agents); (3) ADDING COMMENTARY — comments such as 'ESBL detected — cephalosporins clinically unreliable despite apparent in-vitro susceptibility' or 'confirm clinical indication before treating — potential ASB' guide clinical decision-making directly from the laboratory report; (4) RAPID RESULT COMMUNICATION — proactively contacting clinical teams for critical resistance patterns (e.g. positive blood culture with carbapenem resistance, positive MRSA screen) rather than waiting for results to be retrieved." }
